+++ b/vendor/README.md
@@ -1,5 +1,24 @@
 # WebSocket decoder storage and scheduling patch
 
+This isolated experiment additionally supports completion I/O. After
+`enable_external_read`, a blocked decoder's `take_external_read` transfers its
+actual receive allocation and admission lease to one pending read. Parsing and
+storage lending remain blocked until `restore_external_read` returns that same
+storage. `ReadStorage::take_io_buffer` and `restore_io_buffer` let an owned I/O
+wrapper retain both buffer and lease through the completion, including cancelled
+reads. Published `Bytes` and the framing/fragment/control state stay in place.
+EOF is restored explicitly. The relay's small audited buffer adapter implements
+Monoio's unsafe buffer traits; the relay and decoder APIs require no unsafe code.
+
+`set_external_read_standby_capacity(14)` reserves one maximum-size WebSocket
+header for an idle socket, with the normal twice-capacity admission allowance.
+Body growth still reserves before allocation and may move unfinished input.
+Between frames an oversized slab releases before a later small value is routed;
+only an incomplete header can be copied into the standby slab. Periodic receive
+reclamation first cancels and awaits any active read, then resumes the existing
+reclaim method. This experiment uses normal io_uring receive/writev operations;
+it does not claim kernel-to-userspace zero-copy networking.
+
 The relay uses the published `tungstenite` and `tokio-tungstenite` **0.30.0**
 sources with explicit storage reclamation, admission and bounded parsing APIs. The native
 connection can release unused decoder storage after a quiet input interval
